Stat 5810 Applied Spatial Statistics                                 Fall 2000

                      How to Solve a Problem in S-Plus
                      ================================

                         Mon 10/9/00 & Wed 10/11/00


Dr. Juergen Symanzik

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Write S-Plus functions that implement the quadrat plot
from Section 2.3.1. One function might be used to do the
binning, i.e., to count how many points fall in each quadrat.
Another function might be used to create a 3D plot. 
Instead of drawing a full 3D histogram, you can
simply work with `persp'. The user should call only the
main function with 3 input parameters, where xbins and
ybins are optional:

  spp: a nx2 matrix of xy-coordinates representing a spatial point pattern
  xbins: number of bins >= 1 in x direction, default = 10
  ybins: number of bins >= 1 in y direction, default = 10

Test your functions with the quakes.bay data set. Also create
some data from a bivariate Normal distribution and use this
to check your result.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

How to proceed:

1) For the binning function:

a) test your input parameters
b) set up a matrix that contains the counts
c) loop through your data and count the number of events
   in each bin - this can be done as follows:

   let
      xmin = smallest observed x-value
      xmax = largest observed x-value (assume xmin < xmax)
      deltax = xmax - xmin
      xbins = number of available bins (e.g., 5)

   assign x to the following bin:
      trunc ( (x - xmin) / deltax * xbins + 1)   if x < xmax
      xbins   if x = xmax

   do the same for the y-direction
d) test your function !!!


2) For the plotting function:

a) call persp
b) test your function !!!


3) Using the quakes.bay data set and data from a Normal distribution:

a) extract longitude and latitude as x & y coordinates
b) create the spp matrix
c) call the function
d) test your functions !!

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1) Coding of the binning function:

binning _ function (spp, xbins = 10, ybins = 10)
{
  if (xbins < 1 || ybins < 1)
  {
    cat ("xbin and ybin must be >= 1.", fill = T)
    return (matrix(-1, 1, 1))
  }

  bincount _ matrix(0, xbins, ybins)

  x _ spp[,1]
  y _ spp[,2]

  xmin _ min (x)
  xmax _ max (x)
  ymin _ min (y)
  ymax _ max (y)

  deltax _ xmax - xmin
  deltay _ ymax - ymin

  for (i in 1:nrow(spp))
  {
    xx _ ifelse (x[i] == xmax, xbins, trunc ( (x[i] - xmin) / deltax * xbins + 1))
    yy _ ifelse (y[i] == ymax, ybins, trunc ( (y[i] - ymin) / deltay * ybins + 1))
    bincount[xx, yy] _ bincount[xx, yy] + 1
  }

  cat (sum(bincount))
  cat (" events have been binned.", fill = T)

  return (bincount)
}


2) Coding of the drawing function:


drawquadrats _ function (spp, xbins = 10, ybins = 10)
{
  binmat _ binning (spp, xbins, ybins)
  if (binmat[1,1] == -1)
    cat ("No plot possible.", fill = T)
  else
    persp (binmat)
  "OK"
}


3) Using the quakes.bay data set:

x _ quakes.bay$longitude
y _ quakes.bay$latitude
spp _ matrix (c(x, y), length(x), 2)

drawquadrats (spp)
drawquadrats (spp, 10, 15)

par (mfrow = c(1, 2))
plot (quakes.spp)
drawquadrats (spp)


4) Testing:

x _ quakes.bay$longitude
y _ quakes.bay$latitude

x _ x[1:100]
y _ y[1:100]

xmin _ min (x)
xmax _ max (x)
ymin _ min (y)
ymax _ max (y)

deltax _ xmax - xmin
deltay _ ymax - ymin

xbins _ 5 
ybins _ 5

ifelse (x == xmax, xbins, trunc ( (x - xmin) / deltax * xbins + 1))

spp _ matrix (c(x, y), 100, 2)
binning (spp)
binning (spp, 0, 1)
binning (spp, 1, 1)
binning (spp, 2, 2)
binning (spp, 10, 15)

drawquadrats (spp)
drawquadrats (spp, 0, 1)
drawquadrats (spp, 1, 1)
drawquadrats (spp, 2, 2)
drawquadrats (spp, 10, 15)


# test a bivariate normal with correlation 0 
xn _ rnorm (1000)
yn _ rnorm (1000)
nn _ matrix (c(xn, yn), 1000, 2)
par (mfrow = c(1, 2))
plot (nn)
drawquadrats (nn)

nn2 _ nn[(nn[,1] < 0) & (nn[,2] < 0),]
par (mfrow = c(1, 2))
plot (nn2)
drawquadrats (nn2)


